WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.220
Shine blue light on cells in a mouse's dentate gyrus, and the mouse freezes.

2
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.440
Those cells were tagged while it learned to fear one box.

3
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.300
But now the mouse is in a different box.

4
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.820
Headlines said scientists had found where the memory is stored.

5
00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:22.580
Had it?

6
00:00:22.683 --> 00:00:24.383
How are the cells tagged?

7
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:29.623
c-fos is a gene switched on in recently active neurons.

8
00:00:30.443 --> 00:00:36.343
The group used its promoter to put an opsin, channelrhodopsin-2, into dentate gyrus

9
00:00:36.343 --> 00:00:37.903
cells active during learning.

10
00:00:38.943 --> 00:00:44.623
Mice were off a drug, doxycycline, for one or two days before training, and back on

11
00:00:44.623 --> 00:00:47.123
it afterwards. That restricted the tagging.

12
00:00:47.123 --> 00:00:50.183
Light now switches on just those cells.

13
00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:57.003
Days later, light on those cells made the mice freeze in a different context.

14
00:00:57.983 --> 00:00:59.743
Two controls made this specific.

15
00:01:00.943 --> 00:01:04.683
Cells tagged in a neutral context did not evoke fear freezing.

16
00:01:05.743 --> 00:01:09.923
Mice with a fluorescent protein but no opsin did not freeze to light.

17
00:01:11.043 --> 00:01:16.463
The pulses were 20 Hz, which describes the intervention, not how the cells fire in

18
00:01:16.463 --> 00:01:21.863
natural recall. So activating the tagged cells is enough to produce the behaviour.

19
00:01:22.563 --> 00:01:24.903
That is sufficiency, in this preparation.

20
00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:29.077
Enough is not the same as needed.

21
00:01:29.837 --> 00:01:31.717
The authors themselves noted this.

22
00:01:32.557 --> 00:01:35.717
Necessity needs silencing during natural recall.

23
00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:42.357
Kitamura and colleagues tagged prefrontal cells in mice at learning, then inhibited

24
00:01:42.357 --> 00:01:46.617
them later. Natural cue recall failed on day 12.

25
00:01:47.237 --> 00:01:49.177
It did not fail on day 2.

26
00:01:49.537 --> 00:01:53.617
So the cells were needed at the later delay, not the earlier one.

27
00:01:54.417 --> 00:01:56.217
So where is the memory?

28
00:01:57.397 --> 00:02:03.397
Roy and colleagues mapped learning-activated cells across 247 mouse brain regions.

29
00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:08.077
They flagged 117 as likely engram candidates.

30
00:02:09.457 --> 00:02:14.437
Reactivating several ensembles together gave more recall than reactivating one.

31
00:02:15.417 --> 00:02:18.697
Where the memory is may have more than one answer.

32
00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:24.390
Keep the rule. Light that triggers freezing shows sufficiency.

33
00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.610
Necessity needs silencing during natural recall.

34
00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:34.530
All of this is mouse fear learning over days, not human memory over years.

35
00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.490
Sufficiency is not necessity.

36
00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.730
Ask which control each claim needs.
